  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>The common hatchetfish (Gasteropelecus sternicla) is a freshwater hatchetfish from South America, described from Suriname. Its distribution is questionable, with records from the middle Amazon, Venezuela, Guyana, French Guiana and the Río Paraguay basin. It spends almost all of its time at or just below the surface. The most frequently seen of the silver hatchetfishes, it is peaceful but nervous and shy.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>This species has a questionable distribution having been recorded from the middle Amazon basin in Peru and Brazil, plus rivers of Venezuela, Guyana, and French Guiana. It has also been collected in the Río Paraguay basin (Britski, et al. 2007).</p><p>Type locality is given simply as 'Suriname'.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Slow-moving forest streams, tributaries and swamps. The fish are most often found in areas with an abundance of surface vegetation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>An aquarium with a surface area measuring a minimum of 120 × 30 cm (48 × 12 in) or equivalent is required. Volume: about 108 litres (29 US gal).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Cover a good portion of the water surface with floating plants, which will help to make this flighty species less skittish. Other décor is not particularly critical, but it looks good in both heavily planted set-ups and Amazon biotope-style tanks, with driftwood branches, a sand substrate and some leaf litter. Make sure the tank cover is very tightly fitting, as it is capable of clearing several metres in a single jump.</p><p>Unlike the species in Carnegiella, this hatchetfish will descend from the surface more often, to interact with conspecifics, feed, or simply remain motionless.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 20–28°C (68–82°F)</li><li>pH: 5.0–7.5</li><li>Hardness: 1–12 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Naturally feeds on insects on and probably above the surface, crustaceans, worms. A study carried out on Thoracocharax stellatus (Netto-Ferreira et al, 2007) determined that 99.6% of the diet consisted of insects, and the majority (87.6%) of these were terrestrial.</p><p>Sometimes a little reluctant to take dried foods initially, but it will usually learn to accept them over time. A large proportion of the diet should consist of frozen and live foods, such as bloodworm and Daphnia. Gut-loaded Drosophila fruit flies make an excellent food if available.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Very peaceful but it can be nervous and shy, and will not compete well with boisterous species for food. Good tankmates include other small fishes that inhabit different parts of the tank, for example tetras and dwarf cichlids, or catfishes such as Corydoras and smaller Loricariids. Always buy at least half a dozen, as it will not settle without the security of conspecifics.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Females are noticeably rounder-bodied than males when full of eggs.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>Unknown. Probably breeds in a similar fashion to the marbled hatchetfish, Carnegiella strigata.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<p>This species spends almost all of its time at or just below the water surface, although it will sometimes retreat into midwater if threatened or feeding. Like other freshwater hatchetfishes, it is renowned for its ability to leap from the water surface and glide for distances of several metres. This behaviour is used both to catch flying insects, and to escape potential predators.</p><p>The family Gasteropelecidae contains three genera: Thoracocharax (T. securis and T. stellatus), Gasteropelecus (G. sternicla, G. levis and G. maculatus) and Carnegiella (four described species, smaller and lacking an adipose fin). G. sternicla is the more frequently seen of the larger silver species, though any of them may be offered as “Silver Hatchetfish.”</p><p>Freshwater hatchetfishes are sometimes said to be capable of propelled flight by beating their pectoral fins, but this was disproven by Wiest (1995) using high speed video: the pectoral fins are used in leaving the water but not while the fish is in the air. The action is a powerful jump, a modified threat response. The fish seem able to jump only once or twice before needing rest. Anecdotal evidence suggests gasteropelecids rarely jump in aquaria, but leaving the tank cover open during darkness is likely to result in one or more hatchetfish on the floor by morning.</p><p>G. sternicla is generally considered to be the hardiest hatchetfish available in the hobby, but it can still be quite delicate when initially imported. Once acclimatised, it proves to be a very good aquarium resident. It is often confused with the silver hatchet, G. levis, and is often labelled as such when on sale.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/gasteropelecus-sternicla",
      "html": "<ul><li>Britski, H. A., K. Z. D. S. Silimon, and B. S. Lopes (2007). Peixes do Pantanal: manual de identificaçäo, 2 ed. Brasília, DF: Embrapa Informaçäo Tecnológica, 1-227</li><li>Netto-Ferreira, Andre L., Miriam P. Albrecht, JorgeL. Nessimian and Erica P. Caramaschi (2007). Feeding Habits of Thoracocharax stellatus (Characiformes: Gasteropelecidae) in the upper Rio Tocantins, Brazil Neotropical Ichthyology 5(1), 69-74</li><li>Wiest, F. C. (1995). The specialized locomotory apparatus of the freshwater hatchetfish family Gasteropelecidae. Journal of Zoology 236(4), 571-592</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
